WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.900
But we're not going to worry too much about the in-depth hacking parts of this today.

2
00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:10.900
But what I will jump into is this relationship between car hackers

3
00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:13.900
and the automotive cybersecurity industry.

4
00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:16.900
So where this all started with people experimenting with cars

5
00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:19.900
and finding ways to hack them and modify them,

6
00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:24.900
eventually an industry grew around this, which is more on the opposite side of the coin.

7
00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:27.900
This is the actual automotive industry trying to implement measures

8
00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:30.900
that will prevent people from modifying the software in their vehicles

9
00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:34.900
or modifying the behavior in their vehicles for security purposes.

10
00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:37.900
So the OEM's objective is to make a secure vehicle.

11
00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:42.900
This means that they're going to invest time into designing secure systems for their vehicles.

12
00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:46.900
Things like the software networking architecture that they're using,

13
00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:51.900
the actual selection of different software versions that they use for different applications inside the vehicle,

14
00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:53.900
following standards and best practices,

15
00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:56.900
designing their in-vehicle networks so that they're secure

16
00:00:56.900 --> 00:01:04.900
and properly isolated between critical and non-safety critical networks and applications,

17
00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:09.900
doing penetration testing, and then producing in a secure way with secure PKI, etc.

18
00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:11.900
And your end result is a secure car,

19
00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:17.900
whereas the hackers, they're interested in damaging the vehicle or stealing things

20
00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:19.900
sometimes if they have criminal intent.

21
00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:22.900
But a lot of people are just interested in looking at the actual electronics

22
00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:24.900
and being able to modify them at their will.

23
00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:29.900
Kind of a consumer rights angle to this too, like right to repair is a big part of this.

24
00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:34.900
But these two kind of industries or communities or worlds operate

25
00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:37.900
kind of like two sides of the same coin,

26
00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:41.900
where the automotive security industry is motivated to pay hackers

27
00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:45.900
who have experienced hacking vehicle systems and these embedded systems

28
00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:49.900
to help them to make better, more secure vehicles.

29
00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:53.900
But there are a lot of challenges that are very unique to the automotive industry.

30
00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:56.900
If you think about how many car companies do you know?

31
00:01:56.900 --> 00:02:04.900
Maybe you know all the big OEMs, Toyota, Honda, Nissan, Ford, GM, Subaru, Mazda, Mitsubishi.

32
00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:07.900
I'm kind of running out of ideas or even like, okay, Tesla, Lucid,

33
00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:11.900
maybe like 50, 60 car brands that you can think of

34
00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:13.900
or anything like the European ones or even more.

35
00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:16.900
But when you think about it, there's for every brand that you know,

36
00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:23.900
there's maybe 10 to 20 suppliers in a huge supply chain that kind of makes up a pyramid.

37
00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:26.900
That's kind of why I've represented it like this.

38
00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:29.900
That supplies the different components to the vehicles

39
00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:32.900
and components for those components that the OEM,

40
00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:36.900
the manufacturer then puts together into what's called a car and then sells it.

41
00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:45.900
So your average person would only really know the brand of the car, right?

42
00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:47.900
That's the OEM. That's the original equipment manufacturer.

43
00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:50.900
They're the ones who put their logo and the stamp on the vehicle,

44
00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:52.900
and that's the brand of the vehicle.

45
00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:55.900
But behind every Toyota, you have a company called Denso,

46
00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:59.900
a huge automotive supplier from Japan who makes maybe 60, 70 percent

47
00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:01.900
of all components in Toyota vehicles.

48
00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:04.900
And then Denso gets all their components from other companies,

49
00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:08.900
like Renesas, like Infineon, et cetera, so on and so forth,

50
00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:10.900
who supply software and hardware to them.

51
00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:13.900
And they all end up going up here to the top,

52
00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:20.900
where you have the OEM who actually has the final responsibility for that vehicle

53
00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:22.900
and the final responsibility for its cybersecurity,

54
00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:26.900
according to most legislation around this in the world.

55
00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:28.900
OK, I need to hurry up a bit.

56
00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:30.000
But yeah, vehicles are very long.
